From fa489e21c352ba78ca83c24cb0f82bbe8a386248 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:30:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Kanban: another gateway's Desktop local-hash chat
 owns the cards its gateway links to it

On the hub every foreign `local-<sha256>` session was a machine session: machine_sessions cannot read
the MacBook's state.db, and a Desktop chat and a worker receipt share that shape. So a MacBook Desktop
chat that adopted a hub card through the courier owned the card itself (the courier sub skips the
check) but none of its descendants: resolve_chats skipped its link, _machine_sub_chats dropped its
origin sub from dependency children, add_notify_sub refused it and initialize_ownership fell back to
creator resolution for an explicit origin naming it.

proven_foreign_chats(conn, task_ids) lifts that suppression only for an exact qualified
gateway/profile/session ref with an unreleased `created` chat link on the card or a creator/dependency
ancestor. Only the chat's gateway can tell a Desktop chat from a worker receipt, and that row is its
answer: its reconcile writes an origin card's `created` link only for an interactive chat
(_ledger_links) and releases links to its own machine sessions, owner-written ones included
(_owner_written_releases). A courier binding or `reason: origin` hub sub proves nothing by itself:
`hermes-hub create-task`/`adopt-task` default the origin to the caller's $HERMES_SESSION_ID, a worker's
own hash inside a Kanban worker, and neither row is ever released (review t_01a1cf8c7b6cae6f). Observer
links, released links, unqualified or profile-less refs, non-hash ids and this gateway's own sessions
prove nothing; no network read, no new store. Each of the four readers consults it only after the
existing machine verdict says machine.

_owner_written_releases skipped every `local-<sha256>` as a worker shepherd, so the chat's gateway
never released an owner-written `created` link to its own worker hash, and with this change that row
would have stayed proof for every new descendant (review t_01a1cf9aab0349a9). It now releases such a
link like any machine session's, and reconcile keeps a released `created` row to a worker hash
instead of deleting it as a shepherd, so the release record is not dropped on the next pass.

Proof:
- tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_foreign_desktop_owner.py (27 cases). With the readers as on main
  (helper kept) 11 fail (worker-created and dependency children of create/adopt-bound cards, explicit
  origin, exact-ref scoping). With the earlier binding/sub-as-proof helper 9 fail (a courier-bound
  worker receipt owning descendants, released links still owning grandchildren, origin sub alone).
  test_chat_gateway_releases_hub_written_link_to_its_worker_hash runs the chat gateway's real
  reconcile on a replica holding a hub-written link to a source=kanban hash: RED at 04d0c87837 (no
  release written), then RED on the second pass with only the release fix (the release row retired),
  GREEN here; it also checks the Desktop chat's link is kept and the hub then proves only that one.
- The six named suites, the new file, test_kanban_chat_links and test_kanban_sync_steady_links: 164
  passed. 23 test files importing the changed modules: 493 passed.
- Hub board (read-only, 2026-11-12): 17 MacBook local-hash refs hold a courier binding, 4 of them a
  MacBook-written `created` link; no hub-local or profile-less ref qualifies.
